#### PR 2 (claude-skills): the unattended budget scales with the PR

Built and tested on branch `pipeline/review-budget`, pipeline 0.9.4.

- **Files.**
  - `rr_preflight.py` gets `unattended_budget` and `budget_text`.
  - `rr_auto.py` gets the budget check, partial stop, heartbeat and per-call timeout.
  - `run-config.unattended.json` and `config.schema.json` swap `max_calls` and `max_minutes`
    for `call_seconds` and `max_minutes_ceiling`.
  - The action gets a timed-out status step, and the workflows get `timeout-minutes` 60 and the
    two org variables.
  - The `unattended.md` budget section, two new tests, and the version and CHANGELOG.
- **Steps.**
  - [x] The budget scales with the PR (Josh, 2026-11-03). A run may take
    1 minute, 5 or 50, the way an interactive /pipeline:review does, and a large PR still gets a
    verdict. The fixed `max_calls` 16 and `max_minutes` 15 go away. How it works:
    - The budget function lives in preflight (`rr_preflight.unattended_budget`). rr auto calls
      it as soon as `plan_groups` has split the change, the first point where the groups exist.
      It plans two passes of one call per group plus a context call each, and two checker calls
      per group, at least four. `calls_cap` is three times that. The plan and the actual spend
      go into `auto-summary.json` and the review body, for example "Budget: 26 calls planned
      for 6 groups, about 16 minutes at 35 s a call. Used 25 calls in 14.6 minutes".
    - Ceiling: one org variable, `PIPELINE_REVIEW_MAX_MINUTES`, defaulting to 55, which Ian can
      tune. Every caller's job `timeout-minutes` is set to the ceiling plus 5. Today that is 60 in
      the caller asset and 30 in claude-skills' own workflow, so both change to 60. A PR whose
      plan exceeds the ceiling isn't stopped at the start. It reviews the groups in risk order,
      sensitive paths first, until the ceiling.
    - Before each call, rr checks that the call fits the minutes left, using this run's
      measured call time. When it doesn't, the pass stops partial, an unchecked finding stays
      unsure, and the review posts as partial: today's yellow "Not reviewed", and
      `NOT_REVIEWED` once PR 1 lands. It lists what it covered. Nothing is skipped silently.
    - Noticing a stuck run:
      1. A gateway call times out at 3 times the measured call time, at least 120 s, instead
         of the fixed 600 s in `rr_gateway.py:37`.
      2. rr writes `heartbeat.json`, with the last call, the count and the minutes, after every
         call.
      3. The job's `timeout-minutes` is the hard stop for anything rr itself misses, such as a
         hung process. A follow-up step with `if: cancelled()` then sets the status to "the run
         timed out", so the PR isn't left without a verdict.
    - Runner lock-up: `concurrency` stays per PR with `cancel-in-progress`, so a new push ends
      the old run. The worst case per runner is one PR for 60 minutes. The plan doesn't reserve
      runners. With four on-prem runners plus boxy, two large PRs at once still leave three free.
  - [x] The per-call time is measured, not fixed. It comes from the org variable
    `PIPELINE_REVIEW_CALL_SECONDS`, else 40 in the config, and the run's own mean once one call
    is done. The body and `auto-summary.json` (`budget_used`) report what the run used, so the
    variable can be set from real runs. A fresh runner keeps no run history, so "read from the
    last runs" became this variable.
- **Hardening & verification.**
  - aws-salesforce-data #56, dry run from a workstation against the same gateway at head
    aa3bef6: a full verdict, NEEDS_PERSON by size, in 14.6 minutes. It made 25 calls for 6
    groups, about 35 s a call. The old budget stopped it at 16 calls with nothing posted.
  - The partial path is covered by a test, since #56 never reached the ceiling.
  - Failure mode: someone raises `PIPELINE_REVIEW_MAX_MINUTES` above 55 without raising the job
    timeout. The job timeout then cuts the run first, and the timed-out status says so.
    `unattended.md` says to raise both together.

#### PR 3 (claude-skills): the `/merge` gate as a reusable workflow

- **Files.**
  - `.github/workflows/mi-merge.yml`, a reusable `workflow_call`
  - `.github/actions/mi-merge/gate.py`, Python stdlib, no PR code executed
  - `.github/actions/mi-merge/tests/`
  - `plugins/team-process/assets/mi-merge-caller.yml`
  - the references
- **Steps.**
  - [ ] The caller triggers on `issue_comment` `created`, with `if:`
    `github.event.issue.pull_request` and a body exactly `/merge` after trimming. It also runs
    on a `schedule` sweep of open PRs with a pending `/merge`.
  - [ ] Get the token with `actions/create-github-app-token` pinned by SHA, scoped to the one
    repo.
  - [ ] `gate.py` reads, through GraphQL and REST, from the base repo only:
    1. The commenter has write access (`collaborators/{user}/permission`).
    2. Every review thread has `isResolved`.
    3. Every CHANGES_REQUESTED review's `commit_id` is older than the head, so a commit exists
       after it.
    4. Every required check is green at the head (`rules/branches/{base}` plus classic required
       contexts while they exist).
    5. There is no `review:denied` label. Or the label was removed by, or followed by an
       APPROVED review from, a human who isn't the author.
  - [ ] When all five hold, merge through `PUT pulls/{n}/merge` with `merge_method=merge` and
    `sha=<head read>`. When any fails, post one comment listing every failed condition with its
    link, and never merge.
  - [ ] Interpolate no comment text into shell. Every value reaches Python through `env:`.
- **Hardening & verification.**
  - Expected: unit tests cover each condition passing and failing, from recorded API fixtures.
    That includes a thread resolved by the author, a dismissed review, a label removed by the
    author (refused) and a head that moved between read and merge (the merge refuses on `sha`).
  - Failure mode: GitHub refuses the merge because of a check. The gate reports GitHub's
    message. The checks ruleset is the real guard, by design.

